#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064003 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1e3f3a-5a64-573f-8fb1-9952fa36a839" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Sine"}] :question/id #uuid "48ec6930-88b7-5aad-9bb7-c5e23e766f80" :question/math-academy-id "e-470" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{9}{4\\sqrt{1-9x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin(u)+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have\n\n$$\n9x^2 = (3x)^2\n$$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x$. Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 3 \\,\\textrm d u=\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u$, and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{9}{4\\sqrt{1 - 9x^{2}}}dx &= \\frac{9}{4}∫(1)/(\\sqrt{1 - (3x)^{2}})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{4}∫\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{12}∫\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{4} \\cdot \\arcsin u + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{4}\\arcsin 3x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064004 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"}] :question/id #uuid "3e89a278-54ab-59a6-adb6-38f43c0fbe9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-471" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{3}{2 + 50x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{3}{2 + 50x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int \\dfrac{3}{2(1 + 25x^2)} = \\dfrac 3 2 \\int \\dfrac{1}{1+25x^2}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^2}\\textrm{d}u =\\arctan u +C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have\n\n$$\n25x^2 = (5x)^2\n$$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x$. Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 5\\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u$, and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{3}{2 + 50x^{2}}dx &= \\frac{3}{2}∫\\frac{1}{1 + 25x^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}∫(1)/(1 + (5x)^{2})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}∫\\frac{1}{1 + u^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{5}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{10}∫\\frac{1}{1 + u^{2}}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{10} \\cdot \\arctan u + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{10}\\arctan 5x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064005 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "cb771ff3-b5a9-5900-913f-df23957760b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-472" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt 2} \\dfrac{1}{2+x^2}\\, \\textrm{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2 + x^{2}}dx &= ∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(1)/(2(1 + x^{2}/2))dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(1)/(2(1 + (x/\\sqrt{2})^{2}))dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(1)/(1 + (x/\\sqrt{2})^{2})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this integral now resembles the basic integral for the inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int\\dfrac{1}{1+u^2}\\,\\textrm d u = \\arctan{u} + C\n$$\n\nWe make the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt 2}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\sqrt 2 \\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u$.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$, and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2 + x^{2}}dx &= \\frac{1}{2}∫_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1 + u^{2}} \\cdot \\sqrt{2}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}∫_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1 + u^{2}}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\arctan u \\mid _{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\arctan 1 - \\arctan 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\frac{π}{4} - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π\\sqrt{2}}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064014 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :question/id #uuid "56860611-869d-5035-bf8f-f568c774d78d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5014" :question/problem "${\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{5\\, \\textrm{d}x}{\\mid 5x \\mid \\sqrt{(5x)^2-1}} =}$" :question/worked-solution "This resembles the basic integral for $\\textrm{arcsec}$,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\mid u \\mid \\sqrt{u^2-1}} \\textrm{d}u = \\textrm{arcsec}(u)+C\n$$\n\nLet $u = 5x$. Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=5 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad 5\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫(5dx)/(\\begin{vmatrix}5x & \\sqrt{(5x)^{2} - 1}) &= ∫\\frac{du}{\\mid u\\end{vmatrix}\\sqrt{u^{2} - 1}} \\\\\n&= arcsec(u) + C \\\\\n&= arcsec(5x) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064460 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be85f47e-071e-570f-92cb-fbee2f0947fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061410} {:answer/id #uuid "c9c5ec91-6dae-5c68-aabf-2369c2f4cf95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061411} {:answer/id #uuid "d21e4bc6-9adb-57fd-aed2-333af51b67fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061412} {:answer/id #uuid "73721344-1d4a-53c7-849c-c4c979de6860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061413} {:answer/id #uuid "786466cd-1f50-5bf1-b009-4fcc531fe655" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9c5ec91-6dae-5c68-aabf-2369c2f4cf95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061411} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8187d0f-e969-5a26-98c6-b8cd07a6a116" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061409}] :question/id #uuid "aa336cf9-b3b8-5d4a-a543-f9cee172da34" :question/math-academy-id "q-19394" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $∫_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2 - x^{2}}}dx$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80ae4e27-fd44-59c4-95a3-19ea734ef266" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}\\arctan (14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061416} {:answer/id #uuid "b34b69ba-1ef6-5818-baa4-336cac499183" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}\\arctan (7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061417} {:answer/id #uuid "04964092-53d7-5fd9-b2a5-0efd74895ed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}\\arctan (14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061418} {:answer/id #uuid "0d681a7a-e503-5ba0-84a2-cee035a46c0e